00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Subtitles added by Hanson Hu

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Episode 05 - The Right Magic

3
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:12,360
Hi, Grandpa.

4
00:02:12,420 --> 00:02:13,720
Hi, Robbie.

5
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:15,140
Can I help?

6
00:02:15,580 --> 00:02:16,740
Yes, indeed.

7
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:20,160
Hand me two eggs from the refrigerator,

8
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:22,900
and I'll make you two fried eggs.

9
00:02:25,060 --> 00:02:26,460
How about some bacon?

10
00:02:27,460 --> 00:02:29,380
I made enough for an army.

11
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:32,080
You going to the baseball game today?

12
00:02:33,060 --> 00:02:37,380
It's a perfect day for it, a little cloudy but nice and warm.

13
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:39,380
Dad and I were planning to go to the game,

14
00:02:39,480 --> 00:02:40,780
but he has to work today,

15
00:02:40,920 --> 00:02:42,600
and my friends don't want to go.

16
00:02:42,760 --> 00:02:44,580
It's not an important game, anyway.

17
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:47,540
Do you have any other plans for the day?

18
00:02:48,180 --> 00:02:50,060
I'll work on my computer.

19
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:53,240
I have a new math program, and I want to learn how to use it.

20
00:02:53,540 --> 00:02:55,000
Maybe you can teach me

21
00:02:55,020 --> 00:02:56,940
how to work on a computer someday.

22
00:02:56,980 --> 00:03:00,660
Anytime. It's really easy, but, like anything,

23
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:02,640
you need to work at it, Grandpa.

24
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:06,420
This bacon is great.

25
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:08,280
I love crispy bacon.

26
00:03:09,040 --> 00:03:10,660
Oh, what are you doing tomorrow?

27
00:03:11,540 --> 00:03:13,060
Nothing much.

28
00:03:14,180 --> 00:03:18,800
Well, maybe your dad and I could take you fishing with us.

29
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:20,740
I'd like that, but...

30
00:03:20,980 --> 00:03:22,980
But what?

31
00:03:23,220 --> 00:03:25,240
But Dad is always so busy.

32
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:29,980
Well, can you come fishing with me tomorrow?

33
00:03:31,140 --> 00:03:33,240
Sure, I can.

34
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:38,260
Robbie says you can't take him to the game today.

35
00:03:38,440 --> 00:03:39,660
I really feel bad about it,

36
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:41,640
but they need me at the hospital today,

37
00:03:41,640 --> 00:03:42,800
in the children's ward.

38
00:03:43,020 --> 00:03:44,400
I understand.

39
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:47,800
Maybe we can spend some time together next weekend.

40
00:03:47,940 --> 00:03:49,140
Definitely.

41
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:50,220
We should.

42
00:03:50,520 --> 00:03:52,420
You and Robbie and me.

43
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:55,380
Remember our first fishing trip?

44
00:03:55,960 --> 00:03:57,260
I sure do...

45
00:03:57,800 --> 00:04:01,140
Well, I've got to run, Dad.

46
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:02,440
See you later.

47
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:06,060
Going fishing?

48
00:04:06,380 --> 00:04:08,040
I'm thinking about it...

49
00:04:09,880 --> 00:04:11,240
So, how's work?

50
00:04:11,320 --> 00:04:13,720
Oh, the usual problems.

51
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:16,620
You're working pretty hard these days.

52
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:18,400
I guess I am.

53
00:04:21,020 --> 00:04:24,460
When did you last go fishing with Robbie?

54
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:26,920
I remember exactly.

55
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:30,800
It was on his birthday, June second, two years ago.

56
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:34,260
We didn't catch anything.

57
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:38,020
Remember our fishing trips?

58
00:04:38,020 --> 00:04:40,860
Yes. I loved them.

59
00:04:43,280 --> 00:04:45,740
Remember catching your first fish?

60
00:04:46,100 --> 00:04:48,360
How can I forget?

61
00:04:48,360 --> 00:04:50,760
I fell out of the boat!

62
00:04:52,780 --> 00:04:54,760
We had some good times together.

63
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:57,160
Yes, we did.

64
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:00,020
Maybe we should do it again.

65
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:03,100
How about tomorrow?

66
00:05:03,640 --> 00:05:05,120
Don't you have to work?

67
00:05:05,260 --> 00:05:07,860
My paper work will wait.

68
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:10,580
Oh, Robbie will be thrilled.

69
00:05:10,820 --> 00:05:12,380
I am, too, Son.

70
00:05:12,680 --> 00:05:14,360
I want to spend more time with Robbie.

71
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:16,060
Tomorrow.

72
00:05:16,580 --> 00:05:18,920
It'll be like old times for you and me.

73
00:05:19,140 --> 00:05:20,820
And Robbie will love it.

74
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:25,160
Well, what's the weather going to be like?

75
00:05:25,660 --> 00:05:28,660
Radio says sunny and mild.

76
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:35,340
Well, I'll tell Robbie.

77
00:05:37,080 --> 00:05:40,960
And thanks, Dad.

78
00:05:41,380 --> 00:05:42,680
Don't thank me.

79
00:05:42,740 --> 00:05:44,900
I'm just being a grandfather.

80
00:06:06,260 --> 00:06:07,600
What's the weather going to be like?

81
00:06:08,380 --> 00:06:10,880
Radio says sunny and mild.

82
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,060
What's the weather going to be like in the U.S.A. today?

83
00:06:18,520 --> 00:06:23,160
In New York City, home of the Statue of Liberty and all those skyscrapers,

84
00:06:23,240 --> 00:06:26,660
and the surrounding area, it'll be sunny and mild.

85
00:06:26,840 --> 00:06:29,240
A little cloudy, but nice and warm.

86
00:06:29,380 --> 00:06:32,420
What a beautiful day to do some fishing.

87
00:06:36,120 --> 00:06:40,160
Down in the Southeast today, the weather will be gorgeous.

88
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:43,920
In Miami, Florida, a popular vacation spot,

89
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:47,600
it's going to be sunny and hot, hot, hot.

90
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:50,080
It's ninety degrees Fahrenheit.

91
00:06:50,080 --> 00:06:52,620
That's thirty-two degrees Celsius now.

92
00:06:52,920 --> 00:06:55,400
A perfect day for the beach.

93
00:06:59,240 --> 00:07:03,140
Not so in St. Louis, Missouri, the gateway to the West.

94
00:07:03,760 --> 00:07:04,960
It's cool and windy.

95
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:08,740
We expect rain there and throughout the Midwest this afternoon.

96
00:07:09,160 --> 00:07:10,960
So get out your umbrella.

97
00:07:14,880 --> 00:07:17,920
And in the Rocky Mountains, it's freezing.

98
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:21,160
It's thirty-two degrees Fahrenheit.

99
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:25,040
That's zero degrees Celsius outside Denver, Colorado.

100
00:07:25,320 --> 00:07:29,860
There's two feet of snow already, and it's going to snow some more.

101
00:07:30,320 --> 00:07:32,460
It's a perfect place for skiing.

102
00:07:37,160 --> 00:07:39,960
Out on the West Coast in Los Angeles, California,

103
00:07:39,960 --> 00:07:43,120
home of Hollywood movie stars

104
00:07:43,240 --> 00:07:44,440
and fast cars,

105
00:07:44,900 --> 00:07:46,060
it's sunny and warm.

106
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:50,240
It'll be a beautiful day for a drive up the coast.

107
00:07:54,280 --> 00:07:57,540
And that's the weather in the U.S.A. today.

108
00:08:50,260 --> 00:08:52,160
This is really neat!

109
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:55,000
When do we eat?

110
00:08:56,580 --> 00:08:58,860
First, we have to catch some fish.

111
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:04,780
In order to catch fish, you have to do this.

112
00:09:05,920 --> 00:09:07,420
Here we go.

113
00:09:07,700 --> 00:09:09,080
That's it.

114
00:09:12,840 --> 00:09:15,700
Then drop it into the water.

115
00:09:15,720 --> 00:09:19,560
All of this comes before eating. OK?

116
00:09:19,860 --> 00:09:21,740
How do you know so much about fishing?

117
00:09:22,160 --> 00:09:23,820
Grandpa taught me.

118
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:26,360
We spent a lot of time fishing together.

119
00:09:27,200 --> 00:09:37,020
Now, the important thing is to get the hook close to the fish.

120
00:09:37,240 --> 00:09:38,040
All right?

121
00:09:38,200 --> 00:09:38,880
Like this.

122
00:09:47,460 --> 00:09:50,000
I think I see some fish right under us, Dad.

123
00:09:50,020 --> 00:09:51,740
Oh, not a chance!

124
00:09:52,020 --> 00:09:53,500
I just saw a big one!

125
00:09:54,680 --> 00:09:55,500
Hi, there!

126
00:09:55,620 --> 00:09:56,180
Hi.

127
00:09:56,700 --> 00:09:57,500
What's your name?

128
00:09:57,540 --> 00:09:58,300
Albert.

129
00:09:58,680 --> 00:09:59,840
Are you all alone?

130
00:09:59,860 --> 00:10:00,860
Yes, sir.

131
00:10:01,520 --> 00:10:02,980
How old are you, Albert?

132
00:10:03,180 --> 00:10:04,420
I'm ten.

133
00:10:06,580 --> 00:10:08,020
Where's your father?

134
00:10:08,220 --> 00:10:09,720
He's up there at the lodge.

135
00:10:12,600 --> 00:10:14,140
Does he know you're here?

136
00:10:14,340 --> 00:10:15,420
Yes, sir.

137
00:10:16,020 --> 00:10:19,300
OK, Robbie, maybe you should watch him.

138
00:10:19,360 --> 00:10:21,000
The water's pretty deep here.

139
00:10:21,040 --> 00:10:22,460
I'll watch him, Dad.

140
00:10:29,560 --> 00:10:30,900
What time is it?

141
00:10:31,560 --> 00:10:34,900
It's almost lunchtime, and no fish yet.

142
00:10:35,160 --> 00:10:37,880
I can go up to the lodge for some hot dogs and drinks.

143
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:39,180
No way!

144
00:10:39,240 --> 00:10:41,500
We're here to catch our lunch.

145
00:10:41,640 --> 00:10:44,980
To catch fish, you need the right magic.

146
00:10:45,560 --> 00:10:46,440
That's right.

147
00:10:46,460 --> 00:10:48,200
I forgot! The right magic.

148
00:10:48,840 --> 00:10:50,520
Do it for Robbie, Dad.

149
00:10:50,660 --> 00:10:51,520
You remember?

150
00:10:51,540 --> 00:10:52,460
Sure. Come on.

151
00:10:52,540 --> 00:10:57,300
Well, first, you have to turn your hat around like this.

152
00:10:57,500 --> 00:11:01,960
Then you close your eyes and say the magic words.

153
00:11:02,500 --> 00:11:06,940
Fish, fish, send me a fish.

154
00:11:11,260 --> 00:11:18,320
Fish, fish, send me a fish... I got one!

155
00:11:18,480 --> 00:11:19,580
See, it works!

156
00:11:20,300 --> 00:11:21,460
It's a big one!

157
00:11:21,460 --> 00:11:23,140
Well, it always worked for me, too.

158
00:11:24,180 --> 00:11:26,320
Grandpa, get the net, please!

159
00:11:34,280 --> 00:11:36,040
Dad, you got one, too!

160
00:11:36,080 --> 00:11:37,080
You bet I have!

161
00:11:37,200 --> 00:11:39,300
Easy, Philip, easy.

162
00:11:43,760 --> 00:11:45,380
Sorry, Dad.

163
00:11:46,500 --> 00:11:50,500
Well, one more and I've got a pair of boots.

164
00:11:52,200 --> 00:11:54,120
You didn't say the magic words.

165
00:11:54,240 --> 00:11:55,340
Robbie's right.

166
00:11:55,500 --> 00:11:58,140
Yes, but you did, and we've got our lunch.

167
00:11:58,360 --> 00:11:59,660
Let's build a fire and cook it!

168
00:11:59,760 --> 00:12:01,640
Come on, Albert, you can help us!

169
00:12:01,820 --> 00:12:03,520
I want to stay here and fish.

170
00:12:03,960 --> 00:12:07,720
All right, but be careful.

171
00:12:16,620 --> 00:12:18,340
Is it finished yet?

172
00:12:18,340 --> 00:12:19,300
I think so.

173
00:12:19,340 --> 00:12:23,000
I hope you like your fish well done.

174
00:12:23,320 --> 00:12:24,900
Burned, you mean.

175
00:12:25,300 --> 00:12:27,720
Hey, I'm a doctor, not a chef.

176
00:12:38,020 --> 00:12:41,440
Help! Help! I can't swim!

177
00:12:41,440 --> 00:12:43,500
Dad! Grandpa! He fell in!

178
00:13:01,320 --> 00:13:03,460
Easy does it, Robbie.

179
00:13:03,600 --> 00:13:04,600
That a boy.

180
00:13:04,600 --> 00:13:05,460
That's it.

181
00:13:05,480 --> 00:13:06,720
He's not breathing, Philip!

182
00:13:06,760 --> 00:13:08,540
Robbie, run to the car!

183
00:13:08,600 --> 00:13:09,800
Bring a blanket and my medical bag.

184
00:13:09,800 --> 00:13:10,800
Yes, Dad.

185
00:13:10,940 --> 00:13:14,800
Now, come on, son. Come on, son.

186
00:13:22,680 --> 00:13:25,020
Breathe, Albert!

187
00:13:35,620 --> 00:13:36,480
First.

188
00:13:37,120 --> 00:13:41,540
Well, first, you have to turn your hat around like this.

189
00:13:45,120 --> 00:13:46,200
Then.

190
00:13:46,200 --> 00:13:50,520
Then you close your eyes and say the magic words.

191
00:13:55,100 --> 00:13:57,320
I got one!

192
00:14:00,020 --> 00:14:02,560
First, then, last.

193
00:14:09,660 --> 00:14:11,640
What happened first?

194
00:14:14,160 --> 00:14:15,600
What happened then?

195
00:14:18,020 --> 00:14:19,360
What happened last?

196
00:14:21,860 --> 00:14:22,700
First, then,

197
00:14:23,560 --> 00:14:24,360
last.

198
00:14:24,880 --> 00:14:28,340
Let's try that again, this time with other parts of Act 2.

199
00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:32,100
First, they arrived at the lake.

200
00:14:34,320 --> 00:14:36,360
Next, Grandpa used the right magic.

201
00:14:37,960 --> 00:14:40,280
After that, Robbie caught a fish.

202
00:14:40,920 --> 00:14:44,580
Later on, they cooked. They burned the fish.

203
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,200
Finally, Albert fell in the water.

204
00:14:49,520 --> 00:14:50,540
First.

205
00:14:50,540 --> 00:14:51,200
Next.

206
00:14:52,020 --> 00:14:52,720
After that.

207
00:14:53,060 --> 00:14:54,260
Later on.

208
00:14:54,480 --> 00:14:55,320
Finally.

209
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:04,440
What happened first?

210
00:15:06,880 --> 00:15:08,400
What happened next?

211
00:15:10,860 --> 00:15:12,480
What happened after that?

212
00:15:14,760 --> 00:15:16,600
What happened later on?

213
00:15:18,620 --> 00:15:20,000
And finally?

214
00:15:21,840 --> 00:15:26,920
First, next, after that, later on, finally.

215
00:16:08,440 --> 00:16:10,360
Here's the bag.

216
00:16:10,380 --> 00:16:11,560
Will he be OK, Dad?

217
00:16:11,660 --> 00:16:12,860
I hope so.

218
00:16:18,760 --> 00:16:19,840
That's it.

219
00:16:20,040 --> 00:16:21,060
That's the way.

220
00:16:21,300 --> 00:16:23,140
That's it. There.

221
00:16:23,320 --> 00:16:26,620
Oh, it's going to be all right.

222
00:16:26,620 --> 00:16:27,160
That's it.

223
00:16:27,400 --> 00:16:29,100
Wrap him in the blanket, Dad!

224
00:16:29,140 --> 00:16:30,980
That's it. That's it.

225
00:16:31,100 --> 00:16:33,640
It's all right, Albert.

226
00:16:33,680 --> 00:16:35,240
You're going to be OK.

227
00:16:36,180 --> 00:16:38,100
I want my daddy!

228
00:16:38,200 --> 00:16:39,160
We'll take you to him.

229
00:16:39,160 --> 00:16:44,060
Easy now. Easy does it. That's it.

230
00:16:46,600 --> 00:16:48,640
Your dad is quite a guy.

231
00:16:48,780 --> 00:16:50,060
I know, Grandpa.

232
00:16:51,120 --> 00:16:52,200
How is he, Philip?

233
00:16:52,300 --> 00:16:53,240
He's asleep.

234
00:16:53,420 --> 00:16:54,280
He's going to be fine.

235
00:16:54,940 --> 00:16:56,220
How can I thank all of you?

236
00:16:56,480 --> 00:16:57,800
Thank my son Robbie.

237
00:16:57,860 --> 00:16:58,960
He pulled him out of the water.

238
00:16:59,020 --> 00:17:00,340
I'm very grateful, Robbie.

239
00:17:00,380 --> 00:17:02,080
Dad saved him, not me.

240
00:17:02,160 --> 00:17:04,040
I'm so thankful to all of you.

241
00:17:05,740 --> 00:17:06,760
So long.

242
00:17:08,380 --> 00:17:10,300
He's a lucky boy.

243
00:17:10,680 --> 00:17:13,560
Well, what do you say we get back to our fishing?

244
00:17:13,740 --> 00:17:15,620
That's a great idea.

245
00:17:15,680 --> 00:17:19,920
Uh-oh. It's probably the hospital.

246
00:17:20,860 --> 00:17:23,000
I have to get to a phone.

247
00:17:23,880 --> 00:17:26,020
It probably means we can't stay.

248
00:17:26,240 --> 00:17:27,380
That's OK.

249
00:17:32,540 --> 00:17:35,140
One of my patients has a high fever,

250
00:17:35,140 --> 00:17:36,820
and I have to go to the hospital.

251
00:17:37,660 --> 00:17:39,580
I'm sorry, Robbie.

252
00:17:39,640 --> 00:17:41,140
I guess I ruined your day.

253
00:17:41,400 --> 00:17:43,340
You didn't ruin my day, Dad.

254
00:17:47,480 --> 00:17:49,740
I understand. I really do.

255
00:18:16,500 --> 00:18:18,100
Why are you back so early?

256
00:18:18,380 --> 00:18:20,180
Philip had to go back to the hospital.

257
00:18:20,220 --> 00:18:21,380
He had an emergency.

258
00:18:21,740 --> 00:18:23,940
Oh, that's too bad, Robbie.

259
00:18:24,040 --> 00:18:25,120
Did it spoil your fun?

260
00:18:25,140 --> 00:18:27,620
No, Mom. We had a great time.

261
00:18:27,840 --> 00:18:29,500
Well, did you do any fishing?

262
00:18:29,820 --> 00:18:32,440
Yeah, we caught lots of them. Look!

263
00:18:36,380 --> 00:18:39,360
They had a special on frozen fish down at the supermarket.

264
00:18:39,420 --> 00:18:43,180
Oh, you really had a bad day.

265
00:18:43,260 --> 00:18:44,900
We had a good day.

266
00:18:45,300 --> 00:18:47,700
Robbie pulled a boy out of the water.

267
00:18:47,940 --> 00:18:49,740
And Dad saved his life.

268
00:18:50,700 --> 00:18:53,000
He's a terrific doctor, Mom.

269
00:18:53,260 --> 00:18:54,620
I know.

270
00:19:06,360 --> 00:19:08,700
Hi, Pop. Hi, Son.

271
00:19:08,760 --> 00:19:09,720
Hello, Dad.

272
00:19:10,680 --> 00:19:12,040
What a day!

273
00:19:12,260 --> 00:19:14,260
How about a cup of coffee, Son?

274
00:19:14,480 --> 00:19:17,420
I'd love a cup of coffee.

275
00:19:23,580 --> 00:19:24,940
How was the patient?

276
00:19:25,340 --> 00:19:26,620
She'll be fine.

277
00:19:27,180 --> 00:19:28,900
Was it serious?

278
00:19:29,780 --> 00:19:30,560
No.

279
00:19:32,300 --> 00:19:35,660
Until today, I was never really interested in medicine.

280
00:19:36,400 --> 00:19:38,720
Well, it's hard work.

281
00:19:39,160 --> 00:19:40,440
